## 9. Frontend Testing and Quality Assurance

I use several layers of verification.

### TypeScript

Checks type compatibility and catches integration errors.

### Unit tests

Vitest validates frontend utilities, components and behaviour.

### Production build

Next.js production compilation confirms the application can be built.

### Smoke tests

Basic checks verify that frontend routes and backend connectivity are reachable.

### Browser testing

Manual localhost verification is required for actual navigation, visual behaviour, interactive controls and real API integration.

### Testing principle

Mock-backed tests and live-backend tests are recorded separately.

Passing a unit test or mock browser check is not treated as proof that every live API workflow is functioning.

---

## 10. Development Challenges and Resolutions

### Backend connectivity

The frontend depends on a separately deployed backend.

I verified the configured API URL and tested live endpoints independently to distinguish frontend problems from backend availability issues.

### Raw backend data

Several initial pages displayed technical fields directly.

Phase 1 introduced cleaner presentation and readable explanations while retaining the underlying information.

### Misleading source attribution

Not every result comes from Qloo.

The frontend was improved to distinguish Qloo-supported information from family-provided details, text matches, type matches and guesses.

### Demo storytelling

A dashboard containing many links was insufficient to demonstrate the product's value quickly.

Phase 2 introduced a guided journey while retaining access to the original dashboard.

### Build-generated Git changes

TypeScript can modify `frontend/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo` during verification.

This generated change is reviewed separately and is not treated as an intentional feature update.

### Mock versus live verification

Implementation patches were tested against mock API fixtures, while actual local verification included TypeScript, Vitest, production builds and a live backend connectivity request.

Remaining live-browser checks are tracked separately rather than reported as completed.

